    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n23128" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">infătŭo</span></b> ,
			āvi, ātum, 1, v. a.
		in-fatuor, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>to make a fool of</i>, <i>to
			infatuate</i> (class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">aliquem,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Phil. 3, 9, 22</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">id. Fl. 20, 47</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Sen. Suas.
				2 <i>fin.</i></span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">verbis, Auct. B. Afr. 16, 1: quemque adulatio
					infatuat,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen.
					Ep. 59 <i>med.</i></span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">hominem,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Amm. 15, 8, 2</span></i>
			. — </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Transf.</b>, <i>to deprive of
			taste</i>, <i>make flat</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">infatuatum sal,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Hier. Ep. 2, 6</span></i>
			.</div>
